; t17_strict.n — v14.1 strictness + casting: `strict` marks a value so it can
; never be coerced implicitly again (a compile error at any implicitly-coercing
; op), the property follows the value through variables and containers, any op
; consuming a strict value taints the other operand variables and its own
; outputs (contagion), and `loose` removes the property explicitly.
; `cast` (postfix, [v type]) is the dynamic explicit conversion and therefore
; always accepts strict values; `_cast` (immediate) is the static C-style cast.
; The strict/loose markers are purely compile-time: with them removed, the
; program compiles to the same machine operations. Accepting paths only —
; the rejecting paths (add/eq/format/if/filter-truthiness on strict values)
; are compile errors, verified separately (see SPEC.md "Strictness").
; Expected:
;   s1: 42
;   s2: 1
;   s3: hello
;   s4: 7
;   s5: 10
;   s6: 2
;   s7: 3
;   s8: yo
;   s9: 42
;   s10: 44
;   2.5
; s1: explicit conversion (parse_int) accepts a strict value; its result is
; still strict, so `loose` clears it before arithmetic.
"41" strict parse_int loose 1 add "s1: %d\n" format print
; s2: structural_equal never coerces; the result is strict, print accepts it
; (format would not — it coerces its arguments).
5 strict x!
"s2: " print x@ 5 structural_equal print
; s3: a strict element makes the container strict; get does not coerce.
"hello" strict v!
[ v@ ] l!
"s3: " print l@ 0 get print
; s4: contagion — the strict value w taints d (an operand of the same `set`),
; but non-coercing ops keep working on the tainted dict.
{ } d!
"k" 7 strict w!
d@ "k" w@ set
"s4: " print d@ "k" get print
; s5: `loose` before use — strictness never applies downstream.
"7" strict loose parse_int 3 add "s5: %d\n" format print
; s6: elements of a strict list are strict inside the callback (param taint);
; loosen the element before comparing. The filter result is strict again;
; length accepts it.
[1 2 3 4] strict nums!
"s6: " print nums@ 'big filter length print
; s7: strict shared counter; the cond label loosens before `lt`, so `while`
; never truthiness-coerces a strict value (shared i++ is an atomic RMW — raw,
; no coercion).
0 strict i!
'cond 'body while
"s7: " print i@ loose print
; s8: strictness crosses call boundaries — the argument taints the callee's
; parameter, and the callee's strict return taints the call result.
"yo" strict m!
"s8: " print m@ _call echo print
; s9: `cast` is the dynamic explicit coercer: a strict string parses; the
; result stays strict, and print accepts it.
"42" strict int cast
"s9: " print print
; s10: ...and because the result is strict, `loose` before arithmetic.
"42" strict int cast loose 2 add "s10: %d\n" format print
; static cast: `_cast float` on a literal folds at parse time.
2 _cast float 0.5 add print
ret
big: n!
  n@ loose 2 gt ret
cond:
  ret i@ loose 3 lt
body:
  i++
  ret
echo: a!
  ret a@
